= pH =

== Overview ==
'''pH''' is a logarithmic quantity used to express hydrogen-ion activity in a solution.

== Definition ==
IUPAC defines pH in terms of the activity of hydrogen(1+) ions: pH = −lg a(H+). In introductory contexts it is often approximated using hydrogen-ion concentration, but activity is the formal quantity.

== Scope & importance ==
pH is a compact way to describe acid-base conditions and strongly influences chemical equilibria, reaction rates, solubility, biological processes, corrosion, and measurement systems.

== Core concepts ==
Because the scale is logarithmic, a change of one pH unit corresponds to a tenfold change in the relevant hydrogen-ion activity. Practical pH measurement uses operational standards and electrochemical cells.

== Examples ==
Solutions with different acid-base compositions can have very different hydrogen-ion activities even when they look physically similar. Buffers resist changes in pH when small amounts of acid or base are added.

== Applications / implications ==
pH measurement is used in analytical chemistry, environmental monitoring, water treatment, biology, medicine, food science, agriculture, and industrial processing.

== Epistemic notes ==
The simple classroom formula pH = −log10[H+] is an approximation. Formal pH involves activity and practical measurement is operationally standardized; precision work should state conditions and standards.

